spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md:36:## T28 — Membership integrity: memberships only through 
authorized server paths
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md:38:Task: A `conversation_members` row is created or restored only 
by a server path that authorizes that user for that conversation (direct conversation creation among the users it 
names, under the existing company rules; room conversation membership per the room's access rules; an explicitly 
authorized room join/rejoin; test seeding). No anon or authenticated client — acting through `conversation_members`, 
`conversations` INSERT/UPDATE (`participants`, `room_id`, `type`), the participant sync trigger 
`sync_conversation_members`, the conversation resolver/join routes, or any other RPC, view, or trigger — can make 
itself or any other user a member it is not authorized to be, or move a membership to another conversation or user. A 
removed membership stays removed when unrelated writes happen (another user's join, a participants sync, 
preference/archive/read writes); only an authorized rejoin restores it. A grant (creation, join, rejoin — current 
service-role paths and legacy user-scoped writes alike) that races a committed revocation of that user's access (room 
permission, membership removal) creates no membership: authorization must still hold when the membership is written. 
Preference, archive, and read writes update an existing membership and never create one; a membership that vanished 
between authorization and the write is answered as not a participant with no row created. A client DELETE of its own 
membership has one enforced meaning — either it is refused, or it is a leave that is distinguished from administrative 
revocation and whose rejoin follows the normal authorization — and no client can delete another user's membership; the 
choice is recorded in TRACK. The hosted build and the Phase 4 build keep creating direct and room conversations, 
joining rooms, and changing preferences/archive/read state (including their user-scoped writes, e.g. preference 
