No regression of the r1 resolutions was found.

Two new Low findings:

1. **Lock commentary overstates when acquisition fails.** [Migration:140](/E:/projects/virtual-office/supabase/migratio
ns/20261007184118_conversation_membership_integrity.sql:140). An application transaction performing a plain users 
SELECT can remain active while the migration acquires users `EXCLUSIVE`: its `ACCESS SHARE` lock is compatible. Thus 
“any … table … in use” does not invariably produce `55P03`. **Smallest fix:** say “if a conflicting lock is held on 
any of these tables.” This is documentation imprecision, with no integrity impact. [PostgreSQL lock 
modes](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/15/explicit-locking.html)

2. **A new test is solely a source assertion.** 
[Test:1076](/E:/projects/virtual-office/__tests__/messaging-db/conversation-membership-integrity.test.ts:1076). This 
case compares strings without exercising database behavior, contrary to the repository’s integration/E2E-only test 
policy. **Smallest fix:** retain the statement-shape assertion inside the real database lock helper or concurrency 
cases and remove the standalone source-only test.

The remaining lock attacks did not expose a blocker:

- The backfill writes only revocations. Their installed FK checks reference users and conversations; selecting those 
parents does not fire their spaces/companies FK checks or write triggers.
- Trigger/policy relation locks are covered. Function creation defines bodies without executing their application 
queries. Installed Supabase DDL hooks update the GraphQL sequence and notify PostgREST; no further wait on ordinary 
application DML was identified. Concurrent administrative DDL remains outside this guarantee.
- `NOWAIT` can keep refusing under continuous traffic; it provides no eventual-success guarantee. Retrying the whole 
transaction at low traffic is compatible with T23’s transactional method. [PostgreSQL 
`LOCK`](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/15/sql-lock.html)
- Bounded retries preserve the first held-contention assertion and reject every non-`55P03` refusal. They prove 
eventual **opening-lock acquisition in those local scenarios**, not full-file application or success under sustained 
traffic.

Local read-only checks confirmed the migration-history lock statements, all 13 T28 function bodies, preserved T1–T4 
bodies and RPC ACLs, and both rollback function bodies matching their predecessors. Reported suites, concurrency 
scripts, and rollback/reapply were not rerun. No files or database state changed; no online database was accessed.
